    "grn_variance_user_prompt": "# Objective\nYour task is to:\n1. Classify the product as perishable, non-perishable, or ambiguous based on material description.\n2. Calculate the appropriate threshold based on product type and historical data availability.\n3. Calculate the current variance (in percentage).\n4. Provide a clear **justification** for the threshold calculated.\n\n# Inputs:\nYou will receive the following inputs from upstream systems:\n1. Material description (materialDescription): The description of the product as present in the invoice.\n2. Invoice quantity (invoiceQuantity): The quantity ordered as per the invoice.\n3. GRN quantity (grnQuantity): The quantity received as per the goods receipt note.\n4. GRN Variance (grnVariance): The variance between the invoiceQuantity and grnQuantity. This is calculated as abs((grnQuantity - invoiceQuantity)/grnQuantity).\n5. Unit of Measure (uom): The unit in which the quantities are measured (e.g., KG, BOX, LTR, EA).\n6. Country code (country): The country where the transaction took place.\n7. Invoice date (invoiceDate): The date when the invoice was generated.\n8. Historical Data EDA (edaReport): A json containing the Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) report of historical data for the same vendor/product combination, including:\n    8.1 n_records: Number of historical records.\n    8.2 Global variance percentiles (grnVariancePercentiles): A json with 5th, 25th, 50th, 75th, and 95th percentiles.\n    8.3 Global max variance (grnVarianceMax): Maximum variance value in the historical data.\n    8.4 Historical trends over time (grnVarianceMonthWise): A json with month-wise quantiles of variance, month-wise max variance and count of records.\n\n# Steps to follow:\n## Step 1: Product Classification\nClassify the product as perishable, non-perishable, or ambiguous based on the material description. Look for:\n\n**Perishable items examples:**\n- Fresh produce: \"Fresh Apples\", \"Bananas\", \"Lettuce\", \"Tomatoes\", \"Onions\"\n- Dairy products: \"Milk\", \"Cheese\", \"Yogurt\", \"Butter\", \"Cream\"\n- Meat/Seafood: \"Chicken\", \"Beef\", \"Fish\", \"Prawns\", \"Mutton\"\n- Bakery items: \"Bread\", \"Pastries\", \"Cakes\"\n- Frozen foods: \"Frozen Veg\", \"Ice Cream\"\n\n**Non-perishable items examples:**\nCleaning supplies: \"Detergent\", \"Soap\", \"Sanitizer\"\n- Paper products: \"Tissues\", \"Napkins\", \"Toilet Paper\"\n- Preserved items with clear shelf-stable indication: \"Dried Fruits\", \"Instant Coffee\", \"Tea Bags\"\n\n**Ambiguous items examples:**\n- Canned products without clear content description: \"Canned Product ABC\", \"Can XYZ\"\n- Extract products without clear formulation: \"Extract 123\", \"Vanilla Extract Type A\"\n- Products with batch/version numbers only: \"Product Batch#12345\", \"Item SKU789\"\n- Unclear processing state: \"Tomato Product\", \"Milk Compound\", \"Meat Preparation\"\n\n**Special Classification Rules:**\n- **Canned products**: Only classify as non-perishable if the content is clearly identifiable (e.g., \"Canned Beans\", \"Canned Tomato Sauce\"). If description is vague like \"Canned Product XYZ\", classify as ambiguous.\n- **Extract products**: Only classify as perishable/non-perishable if the specific type and formulation are clear (e.g., \"Fresh Vanilla Extract\" vs \"Dried Spice Extract\"). Otherwise, classify as ambiguous.\n- **Products with technical identifiers**: When description contains mainly batch numbers, codes, or technical identifiers without clear product nature, classify as ambiguous.\n\n**Important:** Even if abbreviations, short forms, or quantity indicators are present (e.g., \"Fr. Appl 2kg\", \"Mlk 1L\", \"Chkn Brst\", \"Det. Pwd\"), identify the core product and classify accordingly. Use context clues from the description, unit of measure, and any other available information.\n\n## Step 2: Threshold Calculation Based on Product Type\n### For Perishable Products:\n- Look at historical data for the **same month** from the previous year (using grnVarianceMonthWise)\n- If samples for that month >= 5: Use **95th percentile** variance as threshold\n- If samples for that month < 5: Use **maximum variance** from available monthly samples as threshold\n\n### For Non-Perishable Products:\n- Look at **entire annual historical data** (using grnVariancePercentiles)\n- If total samples >= 15: Use **95th percentile** variance as threshold\n- If total samples < 15: Use **maximum variance** (grnVarianceMax) as threshold\n\n### For Ambiguous Products:\n- Calculate **both thresholds**:\n  1. Perishable threshold: From same month data (95th percentile if >=5 samples, else max)\n  2. Non-perishable threshold: From annual data (95th percentile if >=15 samples, else max)\n\n## Step 3: Handle Different Scenarios\n### Scenario 1: Successfully Classified Product (Perishable or Non-Perishable)\n- Calculate single appropriate threshold based on product type\n- Include sample size disclaimers in justification\n\n### Scenario 2: Ambiguous Product Classification\n- When unable to determine if product is perishable or non-perishable\n- Calculate **both thresholds** as mentioned above\n- Mention both thresholds in justification\n\n### Scenario 3: No Historical Data\n- When n_records = 0\n- State no historical data available in justification\n\n### Scenario 4: Insufficient Historical Data\n- When n_records < 10\n- Proceed with available data but add confidence disclaimer\n- Include \"limited sample size\" warning in justification\n\n## Step 4: Calculate Current Variance\nCalculate the current GRN variance using the provided formula.\n\n# Justification Requirements:\n**Must be under 255 characters, crisp and to the point:**\n**Line:** The classification of the product and threshold calculation method with sample size disclaimer if applicable. Do not mention the material description.\n\n**Examples:**\n- **Identified Perishable**: \"'Fresh Apples' identified as perishable item, using 95th percentile threshold of X% from 8 same-month samples.\"\n- **Identified Non-perishable**: \"'Rice' identified as non-perishable item, using 95th percentile threshold of X% from 20 annual samples.\"\n- **Abbreviated Description**: \"'Fr. Tomatoes' interpreted as fresh produce (perishable), using max variance threshold of X% due to limited samples (<5).\"\n- **Ambiguous Product**: \"'Canned Product XYZ' contains unclear content details, calculated both perishable (X%) and non-perishable (Y%) thresholds.\"\n- **Extract Ambiguous**: \"'Extract ABC' without clear formulation details, calculated both perishable (X%) and non-perishable (Y%) thresholds.\"\n- **Batch Code Only**: \"'Product Batch#12345' contains only technical identifiers without clear product nature description.\"\n- **No Data**: \"'Milk Powder' identified as dairy product (perishable), but no historical data available for threshold calculation.\"\n\n**Key Requirements:**\n- Always start with the actual description in quotes that led to your classification decision\n- For abbreviations, show what you interpreted it as (e.g., \"'Fr. Appl' interpreted as fresh apples\")\n- For ambiguous cases, mention the specific description that couldn't be interpreted clearly\n- Include the classification reasoning in parentheses when needed\n\n**Do NOT include:**\n- Future monitoring suggestions\n- Routine process mentions\n- Vendor-specific advice\n- Trend analysis suggestions\n- Any additional operational guidance\n\n# Output Format\nYour output must be a JSON object with the following fields:\n{{\n    \"product_classification\": \"perishable\" | \"non-perishable\" | \"ambiguous\",\n    \"threshold_used\": \"<calculated_threshold_value (do not give % sign, only number in string format.)>\" | \"<if multiple values for threshold, mention NA>\" | \"NA\",\n    \"threshold_method\": \"95th_percentile\" | \"max_value\" | \"both_calculated\" | \"NA\",\n    \"justification\": \"A line summing upto less than 255 characters lines: Line 1 - The classification and threshold calculation with sample disclaimer without mentioning the line description directly.\"\n}}\n\n\n# Input data:\nmaterialDescription: {material_description}\ninvoiceQuantity: {invoice_quantity}\ngrnQuantity: {grn_quantity}\ngrnVariance: {grn_variance}\nuom: {uom}\ncountry: {country}\ninvoiceDate: {invoice_date}\nedaReport: {eda_report}",
    "grn_variance_system_prompt":"You are an expert AI assistant specializing in Accounts Payable domain with deep expertise in Goods Receipt Note (GRN) processes, invoice reconciliation, and variance analysis. You excel at product classification, statistical threshold analysis, and interpreting complex historical data patterns. Your core competencies include: 1) Accurately classifying products as perishable or non-perishable from material descriptions, including abbreviated forms and partial information, 2) Calculating appropriate variance thresholds using statistical methods (95th percentile vs maximum values) based on sample sizes and product types, 3) Analyzing month-wise and annual historical data to determine reliable benchmarks, 4) Providing concise, data-driven justifications that clearly explain your reasoning process, and 5) You always provide exactly justifications that are below 255 characters and are crisp, factual, and actionable, avoiding unnecessary operational advice or future recommendations."
